  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nRadar shows the rain ongoing across the southwestern corner of \nMinnesota is struggling to make it up to the Minnesota River Valley \nearly this morning. This is likely due to low/mid level dry air as a \nhigh sits to the north of the Great Lakes continues to advect drier \nair into the region. Did cut back on PoPs quite a bit for today \nacross the CWA, with the exception of our far southern/southwestern \nMinnesota counties. Clouds will persist through the day again with \nhighs barely touching 60 for the central and eastern portion of the \nCWA and only getting into the low 50s where precip is expected. \nWe'll finally see clouds start to move out overnight, particularly \nfor the eastern half of the forecast area. This will allow \ntemperatures to tumble into the 40s with a few upper 30s across \nwestern Wisconsin. Another cool night is expected for Tuesday with \nlows in the lower 40s/upper 30s. There's still a possibility \nwe'll need some frost headlines across east central Minnesota \nand western Wisconsin Wednesday morning. \n\nTemperatures rebound nicely for Wednesday as southerly flow returns \nwith the departure of the Great Lakes surface high to the east. With \nthis will come a slight increase in moisture ahead of a trough \nmoving across the Dakotas. Western Minnesota still has the best shot \nat seeing some rain overnight into early Thursday, but the eastward \nextent is still uncertain as the feature washes out. As mentioned in \nthe previous discussion, there's decent spread in the guidance \nbeyond Thursday. Overall, warmer temperatures still seem likely \nthrough the weekend (70s) with precip chances increasing area wide \nby the end of the forecast period.",
